```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1, "The index of x in the old array list is more than or same as -1");
```

This assert statement verifies that the index of `x` in the old array list is not less than -1. If the index is less than -1, it means `x` is not present in the array list, which is not expected or desired in the context of this code.
--------------------The code you posted is using a tool called Pex (PexForAll + MSFForAll = Pex/Rocks) for testing. Pex is a popular testing tool used in .NET that aims to find bugs and generate test cases. This specific code seems to test if the given index is within the bounds of the ArrayList, and performs a change of the value at the specified index.

The comments in your code start with "@@@" and indicate that you want to assert certain conditions or states of your code. However, it seems the comments do not provide enough specific information to understand what exactly should be asserted. Assuming that you want to check if "index of x in old array list" is more than or same as -1, here is the corresponding Assert:

```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1);
```

Please note, as this line is testing the precondition, it should be placed before the line `arrList[index] = x;`, not after. 

Also, please note that these assertions will be hit during the execution of your program, and they will fail if those conditions do not hold up. Make sure you have enabled run-time assertions in your project (Debug configuration), otherwise they will be ignored.
